d Morrison Professor of Population\nL21@P1: Studies and Economics, Stanford University. E-mail: wba@santafe.edu.\nL22@P1-2: § To appear in American Economic Review (Papers and Proceedings) 84, May 1994.Inductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem\nL23@P2: Abstract\nL24@P2: This paper draws on modem psychology to argue that as humans, in economic decision\nL25@P2: contexts that are complicated or ill-defined, we use not deductive, but inductive reasoning. That\nL26@P2: is, in such contexts we induce a variety of working hypotheses or mental models, act upon the\nL27@P2: most credible, and replace hypotheses with new ones if they cease to work. Inductive reasoning\nL28@P2: leads to a rich psychological world in which an agent's hypotheses or mental models compete\nL29@P2: for survival against each other, in an environment formed by other other agents' hypotheses or\nL30@P2: mental models-a world that is both evolutionary and complex.\nL31@P2: Inductive reasoning can be modeled in a variety of ways. The main body of the paper\nL32@P2: introduces and models a coordination problem-\"the bar problem\"-in which agents'\nL33@P2: expectations are forced to be subjective and to differ. It shows that while agents' beliefs never\nL34@P2: settle down, collectively they form an \"ecology\" that does converge to an equilibrium pattern.\nL35@P2: Acknowledgements\nL36@P2: This paper was presented at the American Economic Association session on Complexity in\nL37@P2: Economic Theory, January 1994, chaired by Paul Krugman. I thank particularly John Holland\nL38@P2: whose work inspired many of the ideas here. I also thank Kenneth Arrow, David Lane, David\nL39@P2: Rnmelhart, Roger Shepard, Glen Swindle, Nick Vriend and colleagues at Santa Fe and Stanford\nL40@P2-3: for discussions ofthese ideas.Inductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem §\nL41@P3: By W. BRIAN ARTHUR *\nL42@P3: The type of rationality we normally assume in economics-perfect, logical, deductive\nL43@P3: rationality-is extremely useful in generating solutions to theoretical problems. But it demands\nL44@P3: mnch from human behavior-much more in fact than it can usually deliver. If we were to\nL45@P3: imagine the vast collection of decision problems economic agents might conceivable deal with\nL46@P3: as a bottomless sea or ocean, with the easier problems on top and more complicated ones at\nL47@P3: increasing depth, then deductive rationality would describe human behavior accurately only\nL48@P3: within a foot or two of the surface. The game Tic-Tac-Toe might be an inch or so down-it is\nL49@P3: simple and we can readily find a perfectly rational, minimax solution to it. But at the depth of\nL50@P3: Checkers we no longer produce rational \"solutions,\" nor do we certainly at the still modest\nL51@P3: depths of Chess and Go.\nL52@P3: There are two reasons for perfect or deductive rationality to break down under\nL53@P3: complication. The obvious one is that beyond a certain complicatedness, our logical apparatus\nL54@P3: ceases to cope-our rationality is bounded. The other is that in interactive situations of\nL55@P3: complication, agents can not rely upon the other agents they are dealing with to behave under\nL56@P3: perfect rationality, and so they are forced to guess their behavior. This lands them in a world of\nL57@P3: subjective beliefs, and subjective beliefs about subjective beliefs. Objective, well-defined,\nL58@P3: shared assumptions then cease to apply. In tum, rational, deductive reasoning-deriving a\nL59@P3: conclusion by perfect logical processes from well-defined premises-itself cannot apply. The\nL60@P3: problem becomes ill-defmed.\nL61@P3: Economists, of course, are well aware of this. The question in economics is not whether\nL62@P3: perfect rationality works, but rather what to put in its place. How do we model bounded\nL63@P3: rationality in economic theory? Many ideas have been suggested in the small but growing\nL64@P3: literature on bounded rationality; but there is not yet much convergence among them. In the\nL65@P3: behavioral sciences this is not the case. Modem psychologists are in reasonable agreement that\nL66@P3: in situations that are complicated or ill-defined, humans use characteristic and predictable\nL67@P3: * Santa Fe Institute, 1660 Old Pecos Trail, Santa Fe, NM 87501, and Stanford University.\nL68@P3-4: § This paper will appear in American Economic Review (papers and Proceedings) 84, May 1994.2\nL69@P4: methods of reasoning. These methods are not dednctive, but inductive.\nL70@P4: In this paper I will argue that in economics we need to pay great attention to indnctive rea\u0002soning; that it makes excellent sense as an intellectual process; and that it is not hard to mode!.!\nL71@P4: In the main part of this paper, I will present a decision problem-the \"bar problem\"-in which\nL72@P4: inductive reasoning is assumed and modeled, and its implications are examined. The system that\nL73@P4: emerges under inductive reasoning will have connections both with evolution and complexity.\nL74@P4: I. Thinking Inductively\n"
